⟦P0⟧Querying for data⟦P1⟧When executing an ⟦P2⟧ statement that returns data, use one of the ⟦P3⟧⟦P4⟧methods provided in the ⟦P5⟧ package. Each of these returns a ⟦P6⟧⟦P7⟧or ⟦P8⟧ whose data you can copy to variables using the ⟦P9⟧ method.⟦P10⟧You'd use these methods to, for example, execute ⟦P11⟧ statements.⟦P12⟧When executing a statement that doesn’t return data, you can use an ⟦P13⟧ or⟦P14⟧⟦P15⟧ method instead. For more, see⟦P16⟧⟦P17⟧Executing statements that don't return data⟦PB1_18⟧.⟦P19⟧The ⟦P20⟧ package provides two ways to execute a query for results.⟦P21⟧⟦P22⟧Querying for a single row⟦P23⟧ – ⟦P24⟧ returns at most a single ⟦P25⟧⟦P26⟧from the database. For more, see ⟦P27⟧Querying for a single row⟦PB1_28⟧.⟦P29⟧⟦P30⟧Querying for multiple rows⟦P31⟧ – ⟦P32⟧ returns all matching rows as a⟦P33⟧⟦P34⟧ struct your code can loop over. For more, see⟦P35⟧⟦P36⟧Querying for multiple rows⟦PB1_37⟧.⟦P38⟧If your code will be executing the same ⟦P39⟧ statement repeatedly, consider⟦P40⟧using a prepared statement. For more, see⟦P41⟧⟦P42⟧Using prepared statements⟦PB1_43⟧.⟦P44⟧⟦P45⟧Caution:⟦P46⟧ Don't use string formatting functions such as ⟦P47⟧ to⟦P48⟧assemble an ⟦P49⟧ statement! You could introduce an ⟦P50⟧ injection risk. For more,⟦P51⟧see ⟦P52⟧Avoiding ⟦P53⟧ injection risk⟦PB1_54⟧.⟦P55⟧Querying for a single row ⟦P56⟧⟦P57⟧⟦P58⟧ retrieves at most a single database row, such as when you want to⟦P59⟧look up data by a unique ⟦P60⟧. If multiple rows are returned by the query, the⟦P61⟧⟦P62⟧ method discards all but the first.⟦P63⟧⟦P64⟧ works like ⟦P65⟧ but with a ⟦P66⟧ argument.⟦P67⟧For more, see ⟦P68⟧Canceling in-progress operations⟦PB1_69⟧.⟦P70⟧The following example uses a query to find out if there's enough inventory to⟦P71⟧support a purchase. The ⟦P72⟧ statement returns ⟦P73⟧ if there's enough, ⟦P74⟧⟦P75⟧if not. ⟦P76⟧⟦P77⟧⟦PB1_78⟧ copies the⟦P79⟧boolean return value into the ⟦P80⟧ variable through a pointer.⟦P81⟧Query for a value based on a single row.⟦P82⟧⟦P83⟧Note:⟦P84⟧ Parameter placeholders in prepared statements vary depending on the⟦P85⟧⟦P86⟧ and driver you're using. For example, the⟦P87⟧⟦P88⟧pq driver⟦PB1_89⟧ for Postgres requires a⟦P90⟧placeholder like ⟦P91⟧ instead of ⟦P92⟧.⟦P93⟧Handling errors ⟦P94⟧⟦P95⟧⟦P96⟧ itself returns no error. Instead, ⟦P97⟧ reports any error from the⟦P98⟧combined lookup and scan. It returns⟦P99⟧⟦P100⟧⟦P101⟧⟦PB1_102⟧ when the query⟦P103⟧finds no rows.⟦P104⟧Functions for returning a single row ⟦P105⟧⟦P106⟧Function⟦P107⟧Description⟦P108⟧Run a single-row query in isolation.⟦P109⟧Run a single-row query inside a larger transaction. For more, see⟦P110⟧⟦P111⟧Executing transactions⟦P112⟧.⟦P113⟧Run a single-row query using an already-prepared statement. For more,⟦P114⟧see ⟦P115⟧Using prepared statements⟦P116⟧.⟦P117⟧For use with reserved connections. For more, see⟦P118⟧⟦P119⟧Managing connections⟦P120⟧.⟦P121⟧Querying for multiple rows ⟦P122⟧⟦P123⟧You can query for multiple rows using ⟦P124⟧ or ⟦P125⟧, which return⟦P126⟧a ⟦P127⟧ representing the query results. Your code iterates over the returned⟦P128⟧rows using ⟦P129⟧⟦P130⟧⟦PB1_131⟧. Each⟦P132⟧iteration calls ⟦P133⟧ to copy column values into variables.⟦P134⟧⟦P135⟧ works like ⟦P136⟧ but with a ⟦P137⟧ argument. For⟦P138⟧more, see ⟦P139⟧Canceling in-progress operations⟦PB1_140⟧.⟦P141⟧The following example executes a query to return the albums by a specified⟦P142⟧artist. The albums are returned in an ⟦P143⟧. The code uses⟦P144⟧⟦P145⟧⟦P146⟧⟦PB1_147⟧ to copy column values⟦P148⟧into variables represented by pointers.⟦P149⟧An album slice to hold data from returned ⟦P150⟧.⟦P151⟧Loop through ⟦P152⟧, using ⟦P153⟧ to assign column data to struct fields.⟦P154⟧Note the deferred call to ⟦P155⟧⟦P156⟧⟦PB1_157⟧.⟦P158⟧This releases any resources held by the rows no matter how the function⟦P159⟧returns. Looping all the way through the rows also closes it implicitly,⟦P160⟧but it is better to use ⟦P161⟧ to make sure ⟦P162⟧ is closed no matter what.⟦P163⟧⟦P164⟧Note:⟦P165⟧ Parameter placeholde