version to⟦P236⟧another Go type that is a good fit for the column value. For example, if the⟦P237⟧column is a ⟦P238⟧ that will always contain a number, you can specify a⟦P239⟧numeric Go type, such as ⟦P240⟧, to receive the value, and ⟦P241⟧ will convert⟦P242⟧it using ⟦P243⟧ for you.⟦P244⟧For more detail about conversions made by the ⟦P245⟧ function, see the ⟦P246⟧⟦P247⟧⟦PB1_248⟧ reference.⟦P249⟧Handling multiple result sets ⟦P250⟧⟦P251⟧When your database operation might return multiple result sets, you can⟦P252⟧retrieve those by using⟦P253⟧⟦P254⟧⟦P255⟧⟦PB1_256⟧.⟦P257⟧This can be useful, for example, when you're sending ⟦P258⟧ that separately queries⟦P259⟧multiple tables, returning a result set for each.⟦P260⟧⟦P261⟧ prepares the next result set so that a call to⟦P262⟧⟦P263⟧ retrieves the first row from that next set. It returns a boolean⟦P264⟧indicating whether there is a next result set at all.⟦P265⟧Code in the following example uses ⟦P266⟧ to execute two ⟦P267⟧ statements.⟦P268⟧The first result set is from the first query in the procedure, retrieving all⟦P269⟧of the rows in the ⟦P270⟧ table. The next result set is from the second query,⟦P271⟧retrieving rows from the ⟦P272⟧ table.⟦P273⟧Loop through the first result set.⟦P274⟧Handle result set.⟦P275⟧Advance to next result set.⟦P276⟧Loop through the second result set.⟦P277⟧Handle second set.⟦P278⟧Check for any error in either result set.⟦P279⟧
